 # SCARF
-Also called: Scalable Continual Learning Framework, Scalable Continstral Learning Framework for Memory-efficient Multiple Neural Radiance Fields
+Also called: Scalable Continual Learning Framework, Scalable Continual Learning Framework for Memory-efficient Multiple Neural Radiance Fields
 
 ## Overview
-SCARF is a method for memory-efficient continual learning of multiple 3D scenes using a single neural network, addressing the catastrophic forgetting problem when training on a sequence of scenes with access to only one scene at a time and data accessible only once [S1]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [DoF-NeRF](9432-dof-nerf.md) or [NeRF-VPT](11770-nerf-vpt.md) by factorizing the NeRF MLP weights into scene-specific and cross-scene shared components generated by a hypernetwork, rather than modifying the input encoding or using prompt-based tuning [S1]. Unlike [Uncertainty and Reliability](14487-uncertainty-and-reliability.md) which focuses on confidence estimation, SCARF focuses on parameter efficiency and knowledge retention across distinct domains [S1].
+SCARF is a memory-efficient continual learning framework that represents multiple 3D scenes within a single neural network [S1]. It solves the catastrophic forgetting problem that occurs when training a NeRF on a sequence of scenes with access to only one scene at a time and single-pass data [S1]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [DoF-NeRF](9432-dof-nerf.md) or [NeRF-VPT](11770-nerf-vpt.md) by factorizing the NeRF MLP weights into scene-specific and cross-scene shared components generated by a hypernetwork, rather than modifying input encodings or using prompt-based tuning [S1]. Unlike [Uncertainty and Reliability](14487-uncertainty-and-reliability.md) which focuses on confidence estimation, SCARF focuses on parameter efficiency and knowledge retention across distinct domains [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A sequence of scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^N$. For the $t$-th scene $S_t$, the input is training data (images/rays) for $S_t$ and a pre-extracted explicit density occupancy grid for all previously learned scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^{t-1}$.
-*   **Output:** A single unified NeRF model $M_t$ capable of rendering all scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^t$ with minimal degradation on previous scenes.
+*   **Input:** A sequence of scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^N$ [S1]. For the $t$-th scene $S_t$, the input is training data (images/rays) for $S_t$ and a pre-extracted explicit density occupancy grid for all previously learned scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^{t-1}$ [S1].
+*   **Output:** A single unified NeRF model $M_t$ capable of rendering all scenes $\{S_i\}_{i=1}^t$ with minimal degradation on previous scenes [S1].
 
